WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.330
Tucked away in the shadows of Bly Manor are
tons of creepy hidden details, secret ghosts

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.160
and Haunting of Hill House easter eggs you
didn't notice on your first viewing.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.540
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the spookiest things

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.749
you missed in The Haunting of Bly Manor.

00:00:17.749 --> 00:00:20.789
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.420
One of the most rewarding parts of rewatching
The Haunting anthology series

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.019
is discovering all the clues laid out in the
early episodes to the big twists and reveals

00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:30.829
at the end of each season.

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:35.320
When Dani arrives in London, she stops to
look at a map right in front of a huge clock

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.640
or chronograph display.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.579
There are cogs and stopwatches nestled within
the larger timepiece, hinting towards the

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.629
importance of time and the various time loops
within the story that's about to be told.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:50.879
And it also foreshadows the countdown or ticking
clock that hangs over Dani

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:55.929
after she lets Viola possess her in the finale
and the eternal fate that awaits her as the

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:57.859
Lady of the Lake.

00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:02.449
Another detail foreshadowing the final destination
of many of Bly Manor's characters is in the

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:05.290
small painting in Dani's room at the hostel.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.030
There are three famous world landmarks in
the picture;

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.400
the Eiffel Tower, Big Ben, and the Statue
of Liberty.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.230
Big Ben is, of course, in London, where Dani's
haunted journey begins,

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.370
and the Statue of Liberty points to how in
the finale Dani and Jamie leave England and

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.920
go travelling in America where they settle
down together.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.060
As for the Eiffel Tower, that's a pointer
to Dani and Jamie visiting Owen in Paris where

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.170
he opens up the restaurant he's always dreamed
about.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.900
And Dani's room number at the hostel 2-1-7
pays homage to Stephen King's The Shining.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.700
The number is the same as the haunted room
in the novel, and fittingly Dani continues

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.460
to be haunted by the ghost of her ex-fiancé
in that room.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.070
Of course, Haunting creator Mike Flanagan
also wrote and directed Doctor Sleep, the

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:52.460
sequel to Stanley Kubrick's movie of The Shining.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.560
Another Shining reference pops up later in
the children's classroom at Bly Manor.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.250
Look very closely at Miss Jessel's desk where
there are a handful of Scrabble tiles that

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.280
spell out the word "REDRUM".

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.900
"REDRUM" is the creepy message that appeared
in both The Shining and Doctor Sleep, and

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.040
which spells "MURDER" backwards.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.820
This easter egg foreshadows how Peter Quint
will later kill Rebecca when he takes possession

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.280
of her body and drowns her in the lake.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.650
A particularly spooky Hill House easter egg
in Flora's room is the smiley face on top

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.280
of a chest of drawers.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.950
This is a nod to Mr Smiley who turns out to
be the abusive foster father of one of Theo's

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:33.410
clients, as well as a terrifying apparition
that appeared to Theo one night.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:37.890
What's especially interesting about this is
that the smiley face is positioned above Flora's

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.280
Lady of the Lake doll which lies beneath the
cabinet.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.010
In Hill House, the little girl explained to
Theo that Mr Smiley lived below the house.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.790
Similar to Mr Smiley, the Lady of the Lake
is a malevolent monster who emerges from below

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:57.280
the surface and it's why Flora insists that
Dani leave the ghostly doll where it is.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.400
"Put her back.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.030
She stays...
there."

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.569
Another night, just after Dani tucks Flora
into bed, she accidentally knocks into the

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:09.760
Lady of the Lake doll which has made a sudden
appearance in the middle of the floor.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.190
This little incident foreshadows how Dani
will stumble into Viola's path at the end

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.400
of the eighth episode.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.650
And when Dani then invites Viola into her
body, watch the scene very closely because

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.590
you can see how the colour of one of Dani's
eyes changes from blue to brown.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:31.510
Prior to this, both Dani's eyes were blue;
but from this point on, she has one blue and

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.270
one brown eye, a mark of the beast now living
in her body.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:40.040
The same thing happens to Miles in the seventh
episode when he lets Peter take over.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.120
"It's you.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.200
It's me."

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:43.290
"It's us."

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.100
Miles collapses, but when he wakes up, you
can see how this eye has gone from blue to

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.100
brown.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.130
And it also happens to Rebecca when Peter
takes possession of her.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:58.220
Because Rebecca has brown eyes to begin with,
in her case the eye changes to blue.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.090
The drawing with the words "Come Home" that
Flora sends to Miles at boarding school, calling

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.520
him back to Bly Manor,
is a sly call-back to Hill House and how Olivia

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.060
Crain both in life and death told her children
to "come home"/to do just that.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.330
"when I flash the porch light twice..."

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:13.330
"Time to come home."

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.800
"Come home, my love."

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.690
"Come Home Nell" was also a message that Theo
uncovered underneath the wallpaper at Hill

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:20.690
House.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:25.419
There's a deep cut reference to Hill House
when Peter gives Miles his dad's old lighter

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:27.979
and says
"Just don't burn the house down, please."

00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:32.680
This is a nod to how Luke, actor Oliver Jackson
Cohen's character in Hill House, tried to

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.229
burn the place down with a lighter.

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:39.550
And you can spot an easter egg to the Bowler
Hat Ghost which haunted Luke in Hill House

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.770
in the scene where Dani is waiting to be picked
up by Owen.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:46.210
Right next to her, is a hat shop which seems
with a number of bowler hats on display.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:51.740
An early clue that Peter Quint had been possessing
Miles comes when the young boy presents Dani

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.270
with a bouquet of roses in the second episode.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:59.331
In the next episode, during a flashback scene,
we see Peter give Flora the same white and

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:01.849
red flowers in Rebecca's presence.

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:05.580
And the way that Jamie freaks out when she
finds that Miles has taken the flowers

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.360
"They weren't ready to be cut!"
is a subtle allusion to how Peter took Rebecca's

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:11.360
life too soon.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.770
And perhaps also to how he killed Mrs Grose
when he was in control of Miles.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:20.039
As Peter Quint continues to develop his relationship
with Miles, he explains:

00:05:20.039 --> 00:05:21.520
"People are like locked rooms.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.169
They've all got different locks, and you've
got to guess the shape of their key."

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:29.860
And the different methods, or unique keys
as Peter puts it, that he uses to manipulate

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.860
those around him such as Rebecca or Henry
is reminiscent of how Hill House's Red Room

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.289
changes its appearance and function to appeal
to different members of the Crain family,

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:41.129
and lure them into its trap.

00:05:41.129 --> 00:05:43.749
"You found the key."

00:05:43.749 --> 00:05:45.789
"You want to know the secret?

00:05:45.789 --> 00:05:46.789
We are the key."

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:48.350
"But it was always the Red Room.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:53.460
It put on different faces so that we'd be
still and quiet while it digested."

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.560
Building on this theme of keys in Bly Manor,
you can spot a key-shaped key holder by the

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.460
door in Peter's time loop scenes.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.710
There are no keys on it though, perhaps a
comment on how Peter can't escape from this

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.189
time loop that he hates so much.

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:13.439
Likewise, after she dies Viola finds herself
locked away in a never-ending loop until Perdita

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:17.250
finds the key to the trunk that Viola had
set aside for her daughter.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:22.080
And it's no coincidence that Jamie is wearing
a chain around her neck with a padlock attached

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.520
on the day that Dani brings her the rings
they'll wear as a symbol of their love for

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:26.979
each other.

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:30.889
The Haunting of Bly Manor also plays around
with the idea from Hill House that the manor

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:34.809
is alive and we can see this in the way the
Narrator describes it when Dani arrives.

00:06:34.809 --> 00:06:37.919
"..and it yawned open to welcome her home."

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:41.870
"The rooms were larger at night, as though
the house itself had inhaled deeply."

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:47.009
And there's another nod to that when Dani
sees Flora's dollhouse open up all by itself.

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:51.319
The dollhouse is a replica of Bly Manor and
appears to have some kind of enchantment

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:55.180
as the dolls inside move around depending
on where the characters and the ghosts they

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.629
represent are in the house.

00:06:57.629 --> 00:07:00.770
Dani lives the rest of her days with differently-coloured
eyes,

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:05.210
and another detail that represents how she's
not entirely human any longer is the doll

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:07.289
that Flora gives her when they say goodbye.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:09.279
"Are you sure you wanna give me this one?"

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:10.879
"You must have it.

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:11.879
It's you."

00:07:11.879 --> 00:07:16.050
Flora regarding Dani as a doll means deep
down the little girl understands something

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:19.639
has changed in the au pair,
because it contrasts sharply with what she

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:22.349
said to Dani when she first arrived at Bly
Manor.

00:07:22.349 --> 00:07:23.349
"Is that me?"

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:24.520
"Why, no, silly.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.520
You're you.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:26.569
It's just a dolly."

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:31.300
When Peter reveals his plan for him and Rebecca
to permanently take possession of Miles and

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.050
Flora, he explains how the children will be
together with their parents forever.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:36.770
"you're gonna be tucked away.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.449
In the forever house, just like we said."

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:39.699
"With Mum and Dad?"

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:44.319
The idea of a "Forever House" is a throwback
to Olivia Crain's dream home.

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:46.119
"This is our forever house."

00:07:46.119 --> 00:07:51.240
Although after she died, that effectively
became Hill House itself.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.129
"This is our forever house.

00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:54.129
It always was.

00:07:54.129 --> 00:07:56.749
Nothing bad will ever touch them ever again."

00:07:56.749 --> 00:08:00.889
In the sixth episode, there's a flashback
scene where Dominic Wingrave confronts his

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:03.650
wife Charlotte about her affair with his brother
Henry,

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:07.550
and we see a note on the fridge with the word
"hello" written repeatedly.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.539
This is a clever bit of foreshadowing of the
reveal later in the episode that it's Henry

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:14.699
who's been responsible for the silent calls
at the manor,

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:18.240
because this is exactly what Dani says when
she picks up the phone.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.300
"Hello?

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:20.360
Hello?

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:21.419
Hello?"

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:25.960
There's a cunning clue to Henry's illicit
relationship with Charlotte in some of the

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.939
early episodes via an advert for a production
of Hamlet on a London double-decker bus.

00:08:30.939 --> 00:08:35.870
Both the Haunting of Bly Manor and the famous
Shakespeare play feature men who have relationships

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:39.510
with their brothers' wives and children who
lose their fathers.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:44.310
Also, in both the play and Bly Manor, the
dead father's son finds himself tormented

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.110
by a ghost.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:51.569
The Haunting of Bly Manor is the latest in
a very long line of adaptations of Henry James's

00:08:51.569 --> 00:08:53.839
19th-century novella The Turn of the Screw.

00:08:53.839 --> 00:08:58.610
And creator Mike Flanagan has said that the
1961 film version of the story, The Innocents,

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:03.280
was a huge influence on his show,
with Dani's surname Clayton being a tribute

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.880
to that movie's director, Jack Clayton.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.670
Another notable homage to the film is the
prayer-like pose of Mrs Grose's hands in her

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:13.880
opening credits portrait which tips its hat
to the opening credits of The Innocents,

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:19.560
where the children's new governess played
by Deborah Kerr clasps her hands in prayer.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.500
Another seminal moment from The Innocents
which Bly Manor draws on heavily is the song

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:29.880
'O Willow Waly', which pops up repeatedly
throughout each adaptation.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:37.610
Both Bly Manor's first episode and the film
begin with the song and several seconds of

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.400
complete darkness,
with the melancholy lyrics neatly alluding

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.399
to the final scene of Bly Manor.

00:09:43.399 --> 00:09:46.990
But now alone I lie...
...till my lover return to me.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:50.860
Where the elder Jamie waits to be finally
reunited with Dani.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:55.980
"O Willow Waly" is often used to indicate
when Flora and Miles are being possessed by

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.389
Rebecca Jessel or Peter Quint.

00:09:58.389 --> 00:10:03.100
The first instance of this is when young Flora
is singing it when Dani arrives at the manor.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:08.670
♪ Till my lover returns to me&nbsp;♪
"Oh, what a beautiful song!"

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:09.690
"What song?"

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.310
Flora responds that way, because it was Miss
Jessel who was in control and singing the

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.149
song in that moment.

00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:20.529
Miles whistles the song after acting inappropriately
around Dani.

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:27.730
And the music box plays the tune when Miles
grabs Dani around the neck and begins to strangle

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:28.730
her.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.329
"Got you!"

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:32.060
"Jesus, Miles!

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.800
Ease up!"

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:38.350
The tune indicates that Peter Quint is in
control of Miles at each of these moments.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:42.830
In the Netflix show, it's shown that Viola
is the origin of the mournful song as we see

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:47.610
her sing it when she performs her nightly
walks to her daughter's bedroom.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:52.000
Flanagan's series also draws on several key
moments of animal imagery in the original

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:53.000
film.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:57.270
For example, the dove Miles kills at school
recalls the one whose neck he broke at home

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.220
and kept under his pillow in the movie.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:04.389
Then there's the many pictures of butterflies
on Flora's walls and the butterfly hairclip

00:11:04.389 --> 00:11:07.390
that Miles gives Dani that belonged to Miss
Jessel,

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:12.000
which recall the stark image of a butterfly
being eaten in The Innocents.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.350
And there's the painting of a turtle on the
classroom wall, which is a call-back to Flora's

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.920
turtle in The Innocents and,
given the Stephen King easter eggs elsewhere

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.009
in this scene, possibly also an allusion to
the cosmic turtle from King's Macroverse.

00:11:25.009 --> 00:11:30.570
And, of course, the TV series also pays homage
to the Turn of the Screw novella by Henry

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:34.470
James through its use of a prologue
with a mysterious storyteller who name-checks

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.370
the book's title in their dialogue, as also
happens in the original story.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:43.060
"And if a child gives the effect, another
turn of the screw...

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:44.060
...what do you say to two?"

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:48.769
In a deviation from the novella though, Flanagan's
series returns to this storyteller in an epilogue

00:11:48.769 --> 00:11:51.470
which reveals her true identity.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:56.389
The ships on the wallpaper in Miles's room
and in model form are a reference to the repeated

00:11:56.389 --> 00:11:59.370
ship imagery and maritime metaphors in the
novel,

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:04.290
such as the opening chapter where the children's
governess compares Bly to a "great drifting

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.959
ship" lost at sea.

00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:10.950
Miles, Flora, and Uncle Henry's family name,
Wingrave, is a reference to the name of the

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:16.069
family from another of Henry James's short
stories, "The Romance of Certain Old Clothes",

00:12:16.069 --> 00:12:20.089
which the eighth episode of Bly Manor draws
upon heavily for its tale of two sisters,

00:12:20.089 --> 00:12:24.829
their husband, daughter, and a locked trunk
filled with dresses and jewels.

00:12:24.829 --> 00:12:29.009
And Viola and Perdita's surname Willoughby
was the name that Henry James gave his characters

00:12:29.009 --> 00:12:33.029
in the first version of that story
before he revised it to Wingrave when it was

00:12:33.029 --> 00:12:35.279
republished several years later.

00:12:35.279 --> 00:12:41.440
Fans of the brilliant iZombie will remember
actor Rahul Kohli who plays cook Owen as medical

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:46.100
examiner Ravi Chakrabarti
and there's a nice nod to his post-mortem-performing

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:50.750
role in that show in the final episode of
Bly Manor when the narrator explains what

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:52.550
Owen did after Hannah Grose died.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:56.690
"some in the village said that the cook rode
with her all the way to the coroner.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.399
And there, he insisted on cleaning her himself."

00:12:59.399 --> 00:13:03.350
And Owen's love of puns, including the name
of his restaurant, A Batter Place,

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:07.569
"I mean, I've made some good cakes in my day,
but this one is batter."

00:13:07.569 --> 00:13:12.569
echoes iZombie's endlessly pun-loving ways
from its episode titles to its comic-book

00:13:12.569 --> 00:13:14.779
segues to its character names.

00:13:14.779 --> 00:13:20.251
There are, of course, a stack of hidden ghosts
lurking in the background of Bly Manor, and

00:13:20.251 --> 00:13:23.779
to discover over 80 of them tap here to watch
my full video

00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:28.690
revealing all the ones I found including the
Soldier, the Vicar, the Plague Doctor, the

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:31.420
Doll Face Ghost, plus the secret hostel ghost!

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.600
There's also a link in the video description.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.959
So, did you spot any other interesting details
or Hill House easter eggs in Bly Manor?

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:40.300
Leave your comments below!

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:43.790
And if you enjoyed this video, a thumbs-up
is hugely appreciated.

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:48.079
Tap left for my full Bly Manor playlist with
